-- ============================================================
-- AUTO PARRY v3  —  Requiem "Blade Parry".  No tuning sliders.
-- ============================================================
-- WHAT THE GAME ACTUALLY DOES (reverse-engineered from Blades.Default's bytecode string table plus
-- the RaycastHitboxV4 fork it drives, which does decompile):
--     every frame, for each Attachment named "DmgPoint" on the blade:
--         workspace:Raycast(point.WorldPosition, point.WorldPosition - point.LastPosition)
-- That is the whole hit test. Three consequences drive this design:
--   1. The damaging shape is the SEGMENT the blade tip sweeps this frame — not a sphere round the
--      attacker, and not a forward cone. A sphere fires on anyone swinging at air nearby, and each
--      false press costs 5 seconds; a cone is beaten by a lateral fly-by.
--   2. Ray length == the point's per-frame displacement, so effective reach GROWS with closing
--      speed. THAT is the "he slashed from far and it still hit me when he arrived" case — it is a
--      real hit, not a bait, and it falls straight out of the maths below instead of needing a
--      "blade linger" knob.
--   3. Because the blade is a real part we can watch, we never have to predict the animation. The
--      old build guessed a "damage frame" (0.30s) and a "linger" and was wrong per-animation; this
--      one measures the blade directly, which is why every slider is gone.
--
-- SO THE PREDICATE IS: will the segment swept by their blade tip pass within my body radius of me,
-- soon? We track the tip, differentiate it, and solve for closest approach. Nothing else.
--
-- VERIFIED CONSTANTS (live getgc on this build):
--     SUCCESSFUL_PARRY_WINDOW_SECONDS       = 1
--     NON_SUCCESSFUL_PARRY_WINDOW_SECONDS   = 5    <-- whiffing costs FIVE seconds
--     PARRY_LOCKOUT_AFTER_SWING_END_SECONDS = 0.1
-- Parry itself is onKeybindPressed("Blade Parry") — an edge-triggered TAP.
--
-- WHY THE OLD BUILD WAS INCONSISTENT (both fixed here):
--   * "sometimes it doesn't even try" — every press armed a 5s refractory, and the only path that
--     shortened it watched for the Gear.Parried animation on the attacker. That animation is
--     defined but referenced by ZERO client modules, so it never plays: the shortening path was
--     dead code and we went blind for 5s after EVERY press. Now we confirm against OUR OWN
--     Gear.Parry animation (which does replicate), and we only arm a cooldown when the press
--     actually registered.
--   * "parries too early" — it committed on a hardcoded animation-time damage frame shared by all
--     13 swing animations. There is no such shared constant. Now we commit on observed geometry.
--
-- Strictly client-side: memory reads, property reads, and one real OS keypress. No remotes.
-- ============================================================
;(function()

